# Subagent git worktrees (builder isolation)
.claude/worktrees/

# Run output: state.json is tracked (it drives the progress page); the
# generated page and most screenshot evidence are regenerable, so they are not.
# `shots/*` and not `shots/`: git does not descend into an ignored DIRECTORY,
# so a negation under a `.gauntlet/shots/` rule re-includes nothing and
# `git add -A` stages only this file. It looks like it worked. Ignore the
# directory's CONTENTS and the negation can reach inside.
.gauntlet/shots/*
.gauntlet/progress.html

# EXCEPT the shell run, which is graded evidence rather than a regenerable
# picture. "Regenerable" was true and beside the point: a critic asked to open
# these found nine stale shots and a 47-check `route.json` with pre-fix wordings
# sitting where a 74-check run should have been, and — the part that matters —
# `09-bombed-keeps-its-own-exit.png` was a bomb-out screen with NO EXIT ON IT,
# filed next to a record saying that check passed. That is the exact false pass
# the opacity fix was written to kill, preserved as its own refutation, and no
# reader could date it because a screenshot carries no provenance.
#
# The GDD logged this once already (§ the "photographed" sentence naming a
# gitignored artifact). Logging it twice and ignoring it a third time is not a
# policy. `route.json` now carries `capturedFrom` — commit, branch, dirty state —
# so these are datable; 380K is a cheap price for evidence a grader can check
# instead of trust.
!.gauntlet/shots/shell/

# Session C post-merge smoke of the Empire shell path (PR #4 / cbef6e9). Not a
# full verify-shell-route suite and not a bar claim — attached so A/B can see
# what landed without review, rather than discover it only in conversation.
!.gauntlet/shots/empire-shell-flow/

# Same argument for the cut-in run: `frames.json` records eight checks a critic
# is asked to read, and `CutInView.tsx` cites this directory by name — the
# sentence that was false until the pictures were committed.
!.gauntlet/shots/cutin/

# And the cut-in CAP run, which is the strongest of the three: `cap.json` is the
# only artifact in this repository that shows GDD §12.3's refusal condition
# being ENFORCED BY THE RUNNING APP rather than scanned for in source. The
# regexes that used to pin it all survive the mutation that breaks it. A record
# of an executed refusal is exactly the thing the two paragraphs above argue
# must be gradeable rather than regenerable — and it is the record that dates
# WHICH build was played, which no amount of re-running recovers after the fact.
!.gauntlet/shots/cutin-cap/

# And the meet SOUND run, which is the only record of the half of GDD §12.2's
# bar — "pacing AND sound" — that no screenshot can hold. `sound.json` carries
# the wall-clock instant every cue reached the audio layer, per beat, with each
# file's own decoded length, so it is the only artifact that can show a cue
# firing again while the last copy is still sounding.
#
# It was untracked for the round in which it found exactly that: five 180ms
# rattles inside 149ms, from a bar-load loop asking for one every 90. A finding
# that lives only in a console someone happened to read is the same failure mode
# as an undatable screenshot, one channel over.
#
# THE RECORD, AND NOT THE DIRECTORY — which is where this one differs from the
# three above, on their own stated reason rather than on their form. Each of
# those tracks a dated record plus the pictures that record describes. This
# directory is shared by two tools: `sound.json` is the dated record
# (`capturedFrom`, per-check `failures`, a `REQUIRED_SHOT_RECORDS` row), and
# `capture-meet.mjs`'s 21 PNGs and `frames.json` are the other tool's raw dump —
# no `capturedFrom`, no checks, in no required list, checked by nothing. A
# directory-wide negation here tracked the record and un-ignored the dump, so
# `git status` was never empty again and every evidence bundle from then on
# stamped DIRTY. That is the staleness instrument grading itself unusable, which
# costs more than the 900K it saves.
!.gauntlet/shots/meet/
.gauntlet/shots/meet/*
!.gauntlet/shots/meet/sound.json

# And the lift PRESS run, which is the only record that the fix a human playtest
# asked for reached the screens a player actually presses. Its whole subject is
# invisible to a picture: `press.json` holds the computed `touch-action` off each
# live element and the `pointercancel` count under a real touch pan, on the
# played session, on a played meet attempt, and on the replay harness — with the
# neutralised and forced controls beside every reading, so the zeros are zeros
# against something.
#
# It is tracked because the defect it found was a fix declared on a screen no
# player can open, guarded by a source scan that named that screen and stayed
# green. The scan is derived now, and this is the only artifact saying the
# properties reached a DOM node at all: `vitest.config.ts` is `environment:
# node`, so nothing in the suite renders.
#
# THE RECORD, AND NOT THE DIRECTORY, on `sound.json`'s stated reason rather than
# on its form. The three PNGs beside it are one screenshot per arm, written for
# somebody debugging a failing run locally. They are deliberately NOT tracked:
# every claim this tool makes is a DOM read or an event count and none of them
# is visual, and the meet arm's shot is taken after a meet that may legitimately
# have bombed out — so it is not reliably a picture of the screen the readings
# came off. Tracking a picture nothing re-reads is what the paragraphs above
# spent two rounds learning not to do.
!.gauntlet/shots/lift-press/
.gauntlet/shots/lift-press/*
!.gauntlet/shots/lift-press/press.json

# Verification MARKERS, written inside the repo for the same reason and by the
# same incident. `tools/watchdog.mjs --budget` writes one BEFORE it spawns the
# command and replaces it with a verdict only when a verdict exists, so an
# interrupted run leaves an INCOMPLETE record instead of the silent absence that
# reads as a pass. See `tools/verifyMarker.mjs` for the ruling and the two
# incidents.
#
# The directory's CONTENTS, not the directory — the reason is three paragraphs
# below, under `.gauntlet/shots/`: git does not descend into an ignored
# DIRECTORY, so anything that ever needs re-including from in here is
# unreachable if the rule names the directory. Nothing needs re-including today;
# writing it this way now costs one character and stops the next person
# discovering the rule the way the shots one was discovered.
#
# AND THIS DIRECTORY IS NOT THE DURABLE COPY, WHICH IS THE HALF THE PARAGRAPH
# BELOW GOT WRONG ABOUT ITS OWN SUBJECT. "Writing here means it survives the
# container" is true; "and therefore survives a rewind" is false, because a
# rewind reverts the whole filesystem and takes gitignored files with it. The
# durable copy is on origin under `refs/heads/verify-markers/<host>`, pushed
# DURING the run by `--budget` and read by `--markers`. What this rule buys is
# only that an in-flight record never dirties a tree an evidence stamp is about
# to describe.
.gauntlet/verify/*

# The dev-server sentinel tools/dev-web.sh writes and every browser tool gates
# on before driving (tools/devServerSentinel.mjs). It describes a live process
# on this machine — pid, port, boot id — so it is not meant to survive a
# restart, a rewind, or anything else; a stale one is refused, not trusted.
# The glob also covers the atomic-write .tmp beside it.
.gauntlet/dev-web.sentinel*

# Verification logs written INSIDE the repo, on purpose.
#
# The scratchpad under /tmp does not survive a rewind. The thirteenth one took
# a running vitest process and its output file together, so a suite run that
# was in flight came back as neither a pass nor a failure — and a missing
# output file reads exactly like a green one to anyone who does not check the
# clock. Writing the log here means it survives the container; ignoring it
# means it never dirties a tree that an evidence stamp is about to describe.
*.verify.log
.suite-verify.log
